{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For only Og king of Bashan remained of the remnant of the Rephaim.</b> Those whom Amraphel and his companions slew, as it says (Genesis 14:5): ״ויכו את רפאים בעשתרות קרנים״, and he escaped from the battle, as it says (ibid.): ״ויבא הפליט״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: this is Og." "<b>Behold, his bedstead was a bedstead of iron.</b> Onkelos translated: באמת מלך — 'in truth, a king,' that is to say, Og himself in truth. And the reason he called him 'king' was to allude to his primal foundational root, for he was of the family called 'sons of Elohim' who descended from Cain. And he intended to write the word in defective spelling and did not write מלכא (with vav). And you already know that all the descendants of Cain were blotted out in the Flood, and none of them remained except this Og."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:00.230370429Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}